  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>At the far end of the Base Nature François Léotard, Plage du Pacha is one of Fréjus's two official dog beaches (alongside the dog area of <a href="/en/fiche-frejus-argens">Saint-Aygulf/Esclamandes beach</a>, at the other end of the Base Nature but separated by the Argens river mouth, so not reachable on foot from here). It's a peaceful oasis, noticeably quieter than Fréjus's main beaches, with a view of Saint-Aygulf and its surrounding beaches across the water.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Access is on foot only, about 900 metres from the Base Nature car park, via a seafront promenade. The path narrows past the Fréjus military base, which can make it difficult with a stroller. According to the official page of the Fréjus tourist office, the beach is open every day, year-round; dogs are welcome on a leash, with no time restriction mentioned in the official sources consulted. From this beach you can watch kitesurfers in the distance on a neighbouring beach.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: bring water and shade for you and your dog — this is a secluded beach, without the facilities (restaurants, showers) of Fréjus's main beaches.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Base Nature François Léotard itself, with its trails and outdoor activities, rounds out the outing nicely before heading back to the car park.</p>
    </div>

